**Design summary**

| Concern | Mechanism |
|---|---|
| Confidentiality at rest | AES-256-GCM; the only visible information is the combined length (nonce + ciphertext + 16-byte tag), which leaks only an upper bound on the plaintext length. |
| Key derivation | PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-256, 100 000 iterations, 16-byte random salt persisted under `__secret_store_salt__`. A different master password yields a different key, so GCM authentication fails on every `get`. |
| Owner / name isolation | Each secret lives under its own storage key `secret/<base64url(owner)>/<base64url(name)>`. `list` filters by prefix. |
| Tamper detection | GCM's 128-bit authentication tag. Any bit-flip in ciphertext, nonce, or tag causes `decrypt` to throw. |
| Copy-resistance | The (owner, name) pair is fed as GCM **Additional Authenticated Data** (length-prefixed, unambiguous). Copying another secret's blob to a different key causes the AAD to mismatch → authentication failure → `get` throws. |
| Salt tamper | If an attacker replaces the salt, the derived key changes and every existing ciphertext fails GCM authentication. The legitimate user sees `Error`s rather than wrong data. |

